course: dictionary
lesson: 4006
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word garage is a good example. «Lost cats often hide close by, in sheds, garages and under cars» means buildings or rooms where things are kept, not places to sell petrol.
explain: Look at the words around the word. In «In a parking garage you stay close to the adult, because it is dark and cars come round corners», the word parking is the clue. It tells you this is a big building with levels where many cars park.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence. Then try that meaning in place of the word. If the sentence still makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A garage is a building or room next to a house where a car is kept.
fact: A garage can also be a place where cars are fixed, or, in the UK and Ireland, a place that sells petrol.
fact: A parking garage is a big building with levels where many cars park.
fact: A garage sale is when a family sells old things in front of their home.
fact: A running car in a closed garage is dangerous, because car exhaust contains carbon monoxide, which you cannot see.
exercise: «In a parking garage you stay close to the adult, because it is dark and cars come round corners.» What is a parking garage? | A place where a family sells old things | *A big building with levels where many cars park | A small shed where bikes are kept | A shop that sells petrol
exercise: «Giant companies started small too: Apple began with two young men in a garage.» What is a garage in this sentence? | *A building or room next to a house where things are made or kept | A big building with levels for parking | A sale of old things outside a home | A place that sells petrol
exercise: Mrs Hall sold her old toys and books on a table outside her home. Which phrase names this? | Car shed | *Garage sale | Parking garage | Petrol garage
test: «Dad took the car to the garage because the brakes squeaked.» What does garage mean here? | A sale of old things outside a home | A tall tower where cars park | A building next to a house for bikes | *A place where cars are fixed
test: Why is a running car in a closed garage dangerous? | The garage door might squeak | The garage might be too big for the car | *Car exhaust with invisible carbon monoxide can build up | Cars make the garage too cold
exam: «The car was parked in the garage, next to the bikes and tools.» Which meaning fits best? | A sale of old things in front of a home | *A building or room next to a house where a car is kept | A shop that sells bikes and tools | A place where many cars park, with levels
